{"key":"nlp-basics-u2-04","seriesId":"nlp-basics","episode":"u2-04","title":"Generative vs Discriminative Models","fps":30,"duration":200.592,"durationInFrames":6018,"video":{"width":1920,"height":1080,"burnCaptions":true,"brand":"learnvi.in"},"badge":["UNIT 2 · LESSON 4","GENERATIVE VS DISCRIMINATIVE"],"series":{"title":"NLP, Simply Explained","subtitle":"University NLP, one idea at a time, no heavy maths","runningPrompt":"","stops":[{"id":"u1-01","number":1,"name":"The Stages of NLP: From Letters to Meaning","stamp":"THE STAGES OF NLP: FROM LETTERS TO MEANING","topic":""},{"id":"u1-02","number":2,"name":"Tokenization: How Text Becomes Pieces","stamp":"TOKENIZATION: HOW TEXT BECOMES PIECES","topic":""},{"id":"u1-03","number":3,"name":"Text Classification: Teaching Computers to Sort Text","stamp":"TEXT CLASSIFICATION: TEACHING COMPUTERS TO SORT TEXT","topic":""},{"id":"u1-04","number":4,"name":"Language Models: Guessing the Next Word","stamp":"LANGUAGE MODELS: GUESSING THE NEXT WORD","topic":""},{"id":"u2-01","number":5,"name":"Morphology: The Building Blocks Inside Words","stamp":"MORPHOLOGY: THE BUILDING BLOCKS INSIDE WORDS","topic":""},{"id":"u2-02","number":6,"name":"Part-of-Speech Tagging: Is It a Noun or a Verb?","stamp":"PART-OF-SPEECH TAGGING: IS IT A NOUN OR A VERB?","topic":""},{"id":"u2-03","number":7,"name":"Chunking: Grouping Words into Phrases","stamp":"CHUNKING: GROUPING WORDS INTO PHRASES","topic":""},{"id":"u2-04","number":8,"name":"Generative vs Discriminative Models","stamp":"GENERATIVE VS DISCRIMINATIVE MODELS","topic":""},{"id":"u2-05","number":9,"name":"Hidden Markov Models, Intuitively","stamp":"HIDDEN MARKOV MODELS, INTUITIVELY","topic":""},{"id":"u2-06","number":10,"name":"Conditional Random Fields, Intuitively","stamp":"CONDITIONAL RANDOM FIELDS, INTUITIVELY","topic":""},{"id":"u3-01","number":11,"name":"Constituency Parsing: Sentences as Trees","stamp":"CONSTITUENCY PARSING: SENTENCES AS TREES","topic":""},{"id":"u3-02","number":12,"name":"Dependency Parsing: Who Did What to Whom","stamp":"DEPENDENCY PARSING: WHO DID WHAT TO WHOM","topic":""},{"id":"u3-03","number":13,"name":"Transition-Based Parsing: Building Trees Step by Step","stamp":"TRANSITION-BASED PARSING: BUILDING TREES STEP BY STEP","topic":""},{"id":"u4-01","number":14,"name":"Word Vectors: Giving Words a Place on a Map","stamp":"WORD VECTORS: GIVING WORDS A PLACE ON A MAP","topic":""},{"id":"u4-02","number":15,"name":"Word2Vec: Learning Word Vectors from Context","stamp":"WORD2VEC: LEARNING WORD VECTORS FROM CONTEXT","topic":""},{"id":"u4-03","number":16,"name":"Recurrent Neural Networks: Reading with Memory","stamp":"RECURRENT NEURAL NETWORKS: READING WITH MEMORY","topic":""},{"id":"u4-04","number":17,"name":"LSTMs: Remembering What Matters","stamp":"LSTMS: REMEMBERING WHAT MATTERS","topic":""},{"id":"u5-01","number":18,"name":"Contextual Embeddings and ELMo","stamp":"CONTEXTUAL EMBEDDINGS AND ELMO","topic":""},{"id":"u5-02","number":19,"name":"BERT: Reading in Both Directions","stamp":"BERT: READING IN BOTH DIRECTIONS","topic":""}]},"stop":{"number":8,"name":"Generative vs Discriminative Models","stamp":"GENERATIVE VS DISCRIMINATIVE MODELS"},"shorts":{"width":1080,"height":1920,"maxSeconds":60,"cta":"Full lesson: NLP, Simply Explained · Unit 2"},"thumbnail":{"headline":"Artist or judge? Two kinds of AI","big":"artist vs judge"},"segments":[{"id":"intro","kind":"intro","component":"TitleCard","short":false,"cues":[{"id":"c01","start":0.6,"end":8.27,"marks":{},"text":"Welcome back to NLP, Simply Explained. This is unit 2, lesson 4: generative versus discriminative models.","words":[{"w":"Welcome","start":0.6},{"w":"back","start":1.12},{"w":"to","start":1.48},{"w":"NLP,","start":1.84},{"w":"Simply","start":2.2},{"w":"Explained.","start":2.88},{"w":"This","start":3.72},{"w":"is","start":4.04},{"w":"unit","start":4.28},{"w":"2,","start":4.653},{"w":"lesson","start":5.027},{"w":"4:","start":5.4},{"w":"generative","start":5.907},{"w":"versus","start":6.413},{"w":"discriminative","start":6.92},{"w":"models.","start":7.72}]},{"id":"c02","start":8.67,"end":17.516,"marks":{},"text":"These two words sound technical, but they describe a simple difference in how a model learns. Once you see it, a lot of NLP falls into place.","words":[{"w":"These","start":8.67},{"w":"two","start":8.95},{"w":"words","start":9.19},{"w":"sound","start":9.55},{"w":"technical,","start":9.87},{"w":"but","start":10.55},{"w":"they","start":10.75},{"w":"describe","start":10.95},{"w":"a","start":11.51},{"w":"simple","start":11.63},{"w":"difference","start":12.03},{"w":"in","start":12.43},{"w":"how","start":12.63},{"w":"a","start":12.79},{"w":"model","start":12.91},{"w":"learns.","start":13.35},{"w":"Once","start":13.99},{"w":"you","start":14.39},{"w":"see","start":14.59},{"w":"it,","start":14.83},{"w":"a","start":15.07},{"w":"lot","start":15.31},{"w":"of","start":15.67},{"w":"NLP","start":16.03},{"w":"falls","start":16.39},{"w":"into","start":16.83},{"w":"place.","start":17.15}]}],"title":"Welcome","shortTitle":"Welcome","props":{"kicker":"Unit 2 · Lesson 4","title":"Generative vs Discriminative","subtitle":"Two ways a model can learn"},"start":0.6,"end":17.516,"startFrame":0,"durationInFrames":553},{"id":"idea","kind":"core","component":"BigIdea","short":false,"cues":[{"id":"c03","start":18.433,"end":26.822,"marks":{},"text":"Here's the whole idea. One kind of model learns how each kind of data is made. The other kind just learns where the boundary between them is.","words":[{"w":"Here's","start":18.433},{"w":"the","start":18.793},{"w":"whole","start":18.953},{"w":"idea.","start":19.313},{"w":"One","start":19.953},{"w":"kind","start":20.353},{"w":"of","start":20.593},{"w":"model","start":20.753},{"w":"learns","start":21.153},{"w":"how","start":21.553},{"w":"each","start":21.753},{"w":"kind","start":22.033},{"w":"of","start":22.233},{"w":"data","start":22.393},{"w":"is","start":22.833},{"w":"made.","start":23.073},{"w":"The","start":23.473},{"w":"other","start":23.713},{"w":"kind","start":24.033},{"w":"just","start":24.353},{"w":"learns","start":24.633},{"w":"where","start":25.073},{"w":"the","start":25.193},{"w":"boundary","start":25.313},{"w":"between","start":25.873},{"w":"them","start":26.233},{"w":"is.","start":26.473}]}],"title":"The big idea","shortTitle":"The big idea","props":{"kicker":"Key idea","text":"One kind learns how the data is made. The other learns where the boundary is.","highlight":["made.","boundary"]},"start":18.433,"end":26.822,"startFrame":553,"durationInFrames":279},{"id":"gen","kind":"core","component":"Definition","short":false,"cues":[{"id":"c04","start":27.733,"end":36.357,"marks":{},"text":"A generative model learns what examples of each class look like. Then, for a new example, it asks: which class most likely produced this?","words":[{"w":"A","start":27.733},{"w":"generative","start":28.053},{"w":"model","start":28.373},{"w":"learns","start":28.813},{"w":"what","start":29.173},{"w":"examples","start":29.373},{"w":"of","start":29.933},{"w":"each","start":30.093},{"w":"class","start":30.333},{"w":"look","start":30.733},{"w":"like.","start":31.013},{"w":"Then,","start":31.493},{"w":"for","start":31.973},{"w":"a","start":32.293},{"w":"new","start":32.453},{"w":"example,","start":32.693},{"w":"it","start":33.333},{"w":"asks:","start":33.533},{"w":"which","start":34.213},{"w":"class","start":34.453},{"w":"most","start":34.933},{"w":"likely","start":35.173},{"w":"produced","start":35.573},{"w":"this?","start":36.053}]},{"id":"c05","start":36.757,"end":45.258,"marks":{},"text":"For spam, it learns that spam emails often contain words like free, winner and prize, and that normal emails contain other words.","words":[{"w":"For","start":36.757},{"w":"spam,","start":36.997},{"w":"it","start":37.477},{"w":"learns","start":37.717},{"w":"that","start":38.117},{"w":"spam","start":38.357},{"w":"emails","start":38.837},{"w":"often","start":39.317},{"w":"contain","start":39.637},{"w":"words","start":40.117},{"w":"like","start":40.477},{"w":"free,","start":40.757},{"w":"winner","start":41.237},{"w":"and","start":41.677},{"w":"prize,","start":41.877},{"w":"and","start":42.437},{"w":"that","start":42.717},{"w":"normal","start":42.997},{"w":"emails","start":43.517},{"w":"contain","start":44.037},{"w":"other","start":44.557},{"w":"words.","start":44.837}]},{"id":"c06","start":45.658,"end":53.457,"marks":{},"text":"Think of an artist who has learned to paint both cats and dogs. Show them a new animal, and they ask which of their two pictures it resembles.","words":[{"w":"Think","start":45.658},{"w":"of","start":45.858},{"w":"an","start":45.978},{"w":"artist","start":46.138},{"w":"who","start":46.538},{"w":"has","start":46.698},{"w":"learned","start":46.858},{"w":"to","start":47.098},{"w":"paint","start":47.218},{"w":"both","start":47.538},{"w":"cats","start":47.818},{"w":"and","start":48.258},{"w":"dogs.","start":48.538},{"w":"Show","start":49.178},{"w":"them","start":49.498},{"w":"a","start":49.698},{"w":"new","start":49.858},{"w":"animal,","start":50.098},{"w":"and","start":50.618},{"w":"they","start":50.818},{"w":"ask","start":51.018},{"w":"which","start":51.378},{"w":"of","start":51.578},{"w":"their","start":51.698},{"w":"two","start":51.898},{"w":"pictures","start":52.098},{"w":"it","start":52.618},{"w":"resembles.","start":52.778}]}],"title":"Generative models","shortTitle":"Generative models","props":{"kicker":"Generative","term":"Generative model","definition":"Learns what examples of each class look like, then asks which class most likely produced a new example.","example":"spam often contains: free · winner · prize","analogy":"An artist who has learned to paint both cats and dogs."},"start":27.733,"end":53.457,"startFrame":832,"durationInFrames":799},{"id":"disc","kind":"core","component":"Definition","short":false,"cues":[{"id":"c07","start":54.367,"end":61.544,"marks":{},"text":"A discriminative model skips that. It learns directly which clues separate the classes, without learning how the data is made.","words":[{"w":"A","start":54.367},{"w":"discriminative","start":54.487},{"w":"model","start":55.287},{"w":"skips","start":55.647},{"w":"that.","start":56.167},{"w":"It","start":56.487},{"w":"learns","start":56.727},{"w":"directly","start":57.207},{"w":"which","start":57.727},{"w":"clues","start":57.967},{"w":"separate","start":58.367},{"w":"the","start":58.727},{"w":"classes,","start":58.887},{"w":"without","start":59.567},{"w":"learning","start":59.887},{"w":"how","start":60.287},{"w":"the","start":60.487},{"w":"data","start":60.647},{"w":"is","start":61.047},{"w":"made.","start":61.247}]},{"id":"c08","start":61.944,"end":68.679,"marks":{},"text":"For spam, it might learn that the word free, together with lots of links, pushes an email over the line into spam.","words":[{"w":"For","start":61.944},{"w":"spam,","start":62.144},{"w":"it","start":62.624},{"w":"might","start":62.824},{"w":"learn","start":63.104},{"w":"that","start":63.424},{"w":"the","start":63.584},{"w":"word","start":63.784},{"w":"free,","start":64.104},{"w":"together","start":64.584},{"w":"with","start":65.064},{"w":"lots","start":65.264},{"w":"of","start":65.584},{"w":"links,","start":65.904},{"w":"pushes","start":66.224},{"w":"an","start":66.664},{"w":"email","start":66.964},{"w":"over","start":67.264},{"w":"the","start":67.424},{"w":"line","start":67.584},{"w":"into","start":67.944},{"w":"spam.","start":68.311}]},{"id":"c09","start":69.079,"end":74.63,"marks":{},"text":"Think of a judge at a pet show who can instantly tell cats from dogs, but couldn't paint either one.","words":[{"w":"Think","start":69.079},{"w":"of","start":69.279},{"w":"a","start":69.439},{"w":"judge","start":69.559},{"w":"at","start":69.959},{"w":"a","start":70.119},{"w":"pet","start":70.239},{"w":"show","start":70.479},{"w":"who","start":70.839},{"w":"can","start":71.039},{"w":"instantly","start":71.239},{"w":"tell","start":71.759},{"w":"cats","start":71.999},{"w":"from","start":72.399},{"w":"dogs,","start":72.599},{"w":"but","start":73.119},{"w":"couldn't","start":73.319},{"w":"paint","start":73.719},{"w":"either","start":74.039},{"w":"one.","start":74.359}]}],"title":"Discriminative models","shortTitle":"Discriminative models","props":{"kicker":"Discriminative","term":"Discriminative model","definition":"Learns directly which clues separate the classes, without learning how the data is made.","example":"free + many links → spam","analogy":"A judge who can tell cats from dogs, but couldn't paint either."},"start":54.367,"end":74.63,"startFrame":1631,"durationInFrames":635},{"id":"side","kind":"core","component":"Compare","short":true,"cues":[{"id":"c10","start":75.533,"end":77.195,"marks":{},"text":"Let's put them side by side.","words":[{"w":"Let's","start":75.533},{"w":"put","start":75.853},{"w":"them","start":76.121},{"w":"side","start":76.39},{"w":"by","start":76.658},{"w":"side.","start":76.927}]},{"id":"c11","start":77.595,"end":87.546,"marks":{},"text":"The generative model learns what each class looks like, so in principle it could even create new examples. Naive Bayes and hidden Markov models are generative.","words":[{"w":"The","start":77.595},{"w":"generative","start":77.715},{"w":"model","start":78.235},{"w":"learns","start":78.635},{"w":"what","start":79.075},{"w":"each","start":79.275},{"w":"class","start":79.515},{"w":"looks","start":79.915},{"w":"like,","start":80.235},{"w":"so","start":80.595},{"w":"in","start":80.835},{"w":"principle","start":81.035},{"w":"it","start":81.715},{"w":"could","start":81.915},{"w":"even","start":82.155},{"w":"create","start":82.395},{"w":"new","start":82.915},{"w":"examples.","start":83.195},{"w":"Naive","start":84.275},{"w":"Bayes","start":84.795},{"w":"and","start":85.315},{"w":"hidden","start":85.475},{"w":"Markov","start":85.895},{"w":"models","start":86.315},{"w":"are","start":86.755},{"w":"generative.","start":86.875}]},{"id":"c12","start":87.946,"end":99.135,"marks":{},"text":"The discriminative model learns only the dividing line, and answers just one question: which class is this? Logistic regression and conditional random fields are discriminative.","words":[{"w":"The","start":87.946},{"w":"discriminative","start":88.066},{"w":"model","start":88.826},{"w":"learns","start":89.266},{"w":"only","start":89.706},{"w":"the","start":89.946},{"w":"dividing","start":90.106},{"w":"line,","start":90.746},{"w":"and","start":91.146},{"w":"answers","start":91.466},{"w":"just","start":91.986},{"w":"one","start":92.426},{"w":"question:","start":92.866},{"w":"which","start":93.306},{"w":"class","start":93.626},{"w":"is","start":94.066},{"w":"this?","start":94.346},{"w":"Logistic","start":94.946},{"w":"regression","start":95.586},{"w":"and","start":96.226},{"w":"conditional","start":96.426},{"w":"random","start":97.026},{"w":"fields","start":97.506},{"w":"are","start":97.986},{"w":"discriminative.","start":98.186}]}],"title":"Side by side","shortTitle":"Generative vs discriminative, explained with cats and dogs","props":{"kicker":"Side by side","title":"Artist versus judge","columns":[{"title":"Generative","items":["learns what each class looks like","could create new examples","Naive Bayes · HMM"],"tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Discriminative","items":["learns the dividing line","only answers: which class?","logistic regression · CRF"],"tone":"neutral"}]},"start":75.533,"end":99.135,"startFrame":2266,"durationInFrames":736},{"id":"models","kind":"core","component":"Table","short":false,"cues":[{"id":"c13","start":100.067,"end":103.285,"marks":{},"text":"Here are models from this course, sorted into the two kinds.","words":[{"w":"Here","start":100.067},{"w":"are","start":100.307},{"w":"models","start":100.467},{"w":"from","start":100.947},{"w":"this","start":101.107},{"w":"course,","start":101.347},{"w":"sorted","start":101.747},{"w":"into","start":102.227},{"w":"the","start":102.427},{"w":"two","start":102.547},{"w":"kinds.","start":102.787}]},{"id":"c14","start":103.685,"end":107.644,"marks":{},"text":"Naive Bayes, from our text classification lesson, is generative.","words":[{"w":"Naive","start":103.685},{"w":"Bayes,","start":104.165},{"w":"from","start":104.645},{"w":"our","start":104.805},{"w":"text","start":104.965},{"w":"classification","start":105.538},{"w":"lesson,","start":106.112},{"w":"is","start":106.685},{"w":"generative.","start":106.925}]},{"id":"c15","start":108.044,"end":114.182,"marks":{},"text":"Hidden Markov models, a classic way to tag parts of speech, are generative too. They're next lesson.","words":[{"w":"Hidden","start":108.044},{"w":"Markov","start":108.464},{"w":"models,","start":108.884},{"w":"a","start":109.404},{"w":"classic","start":109.564},{"w":"way","start":110.044},{"w":"to","start":110.244},{"w":"tag","start":110.444},{"w":"parts","start":110.764},{"w":"of","start":111.084},{"w":"speech,","start":111.204},{"w":"are","start":111.644},{"w":"generative","start":111.804},{"w":"too.","start":112.484},{"w":"They're","start":112.909},{"w":"next","start":113.333},{"w":"lesson.","start":113.757}]},{"id":"c16","start":114.582,"end":119.283,"marks":{},"text":"Logistic regression, another popular text classifier, is discriminative.","words":[{"w":"Logistic","start":114.582},{"w":"regression,","start":115.222},{"w":"another","start":116.022},{"w":"popular","start":116.422},{"w":"text","start":116.942},{"w":"classifier,","start":117.302},{"w":"is","start":118.102},{"w":"discriminative.","start":118.342}]},{"id":"c17","start":119.683,"end":125.917,"marks":{},"text":"And conditional random fields, used for tagging and chunking, are discriminative. They're the lesson after.","words":[{"w":"And","start":119.683},{"w":"conditional","start":119.843},{"w":"random","start":120.443},{"w":"fields,","start":120.923},{"w":"used","start":121.483},{"w":"for","start":121.803},{"w":"tagging","start":121.963},{"w":"and","start":122.35},{"w":"chunking,","start":122.736},{"w":"are","start":123.123},{"w":"discriminative.","start":123.363},{"w":"They're","start":123.893},{"w":"the","start":124.423},{"w":"lesson","start":124.953},{"w":"after.","start":125.483}]}],"title":"Models you'll meet","shortTitle":"Models you'll meet","props":{"kicker":"In this course","title":"Which kind is each model?","columns":["Model","Kind","Used for"],"rows":[["Naive Bayes","generative","classifying text"],["Hidden Markov Model","generative","POS tagging"],["Logistic regression","discriminative","classifying text"],["CRF","discriminative","tagging, chunking"]],"highlight":[]},"start":100.067,"end":125.917,"startFrame":3002,"durationInFrames":803},{"id":"when","kind":"core","component":"Compare","short":false,"cues":[{"id":"c18","start":126.833,"end":129.286,"marks":{},"text":"So which is better? It depends on your data.","words":[{"w":"So","start":126.833},{"w":"which","start":127.073},{"w":"is","start":127.313},{"w":"better?","start":127.513},{"w":"It","start":127.873},{"w":"depends","start":128.113},{"w":"on","start":128.553},{"w":"your","start":128.673},{"w":"data.","start":128.873}]},{"id":"c19","start":129.686,"end":140.207,"marks":{},"text":"Generative models often learn well from just a little data, because they make strong assumptions about how the data is made. When those assumptions are wrong, though, they pay for it.","words":[{"w":"Generative","start":129.686},{"w":"models","start":130.326},{"w":"often","start":130.886},{"w":"learn","start":131.206},{"w":"well","start":131.526},{"w":"from","start":131.806},{"w":"just","start":132.006},{"w":"a","start":132.246},{"w":"little","start":132.406},{"w":"data,","start":132.606},{"w":"because","start":133.166},{"w":"they","start":133.486},{"w":"make","start":133.726},{"w":"strong","start":133.966},{"w":"assumptions","start":134.406},{"w":"about","start":135.126},{"w":"how","start":135.366},{"w":"the","start":135.566},{"w":"data","start":135.766},{"w":"is","start":136.206},{"w":"made.","start":136.446},{"w":"When","start":136.926},{"w":"those","start":137.206},{"w":"assumptions","start":137.526},{"w":"are","start":138.206},{"w":"wrong,","start":138.406},{"w":"though,","start":138.806},{"w":"they","start":139.126},{"w":"pay","start":139.406},{"w":"for","start":139.726},{"w":"it.","start":139.966}]},{"id":"c20","start":140.607,"end":151.152,"marks":{},"text":"Discriminative models can mix many overlapping clues freely, and with plenty of labelled data they're usually more accurate. That's why most modern systems are discriminative.","words":[{"w":"Discriminative","start":140.607},{"w":"models","start":141.407},{"w":"can","start":141.887},{"w":"mix","start":142.047},{"w":"many","start":142.287},{"w":"overlapping","start":142.647},{"w":"clues","start":143.327},{"w":"freely,","start":143.727},{"w":"and","start":144.287},{"w":"with","start":144.487},{"w":"plenty","start":144.687},{"w":"of","start":145.127},{"w":"labelled","start":145.327},{"w":"data","start":145.807},{"w":"they're","start":146.187},{"w":"usually","start":146.567},{"w":"more","start":147.127},{"w":"accurate.","start":147.367},{"w":"That's","start":148.127},{"w":"why","start":148.607},{"w":"most","start":148.847},{"w":"modern","start":149.127},{"w":"systems","start":149.527},{"w":"are","start":150.087},{"w":"discriminative.","start":150.287}]}],"title":"Which is better?","shortTitle":"Which is better?","props":{"kicker":"Which wins?","title":"When each kind shines","columns":[{"title":"Generative","items":["learns well from little data","makes strong assumptions","can fill in missing pieces"],"tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Discriminative","items":["usually more accurate with lots of data","can mix overlapping clues freely","needs more labelled data"],"tone":"good"}],"verdict":{"text":"With plenty of labelled data, discriminative usually wins"}},"start":126.833,"end":151.152,"startFrame":3805,"durationInFrames":757},{"id":"quiz","kind":"quiz","component":"Quiz","short":true,"cues":[{"id":"c21","start":152.067,"end":160.483,"marks":{},"text":"Quick quiz. A model learns only the line that separates spam from normal mail, and nothing about how emails are written. What kind is it?","words":[{"w":"Quick","start":152.307},{"w":"quiz.","start":152.547},{"w":"A","start":152.787},{"w":"model","start":152.987},{"w":"learns","start":153.427},{"w":"only","start":153.867},{"w":"the","start":154.107},{"w":"line","start":154.307},{"w":"that","start":154.627},{"w":"separates","start":154.787},{"w":"spam","start":155.287},{"w":"from","start":155.787},{"w":"normal","start":155.987},{"w":"mail,","start":156.427},{"w":"and","start":156.867},{"w":"nothing","start":157.107},{"w":"about","start":157.507},{"w":"how","start":157.747},{"w":"emails","start":158.167},{"w":"are","start":158.587},{"w":"written.","start":158.787},{"w":"What","start":159.387},{"w":"kind","start":159.747},{"w":"is","start":160.027},{"w":"it?","start":160.227}]},{"id":"c22","start":160.883,"end":162.403,"marks":{},"text":"Pause the video and choose.","words":[{"w":"Pause","start":160.883},{"w":"the","start":161.163},{"w":"video","start":161.423},{"w":"and","start":161.683},{"w":"choose.","start":161.883}]},{"id":"c23","start":165.803,"end":171.011,"marks":{},"text":"It's discriminative. It learns the boundary between the classes, not how the data is made.","words":[{"w":"It's","start":165.803},{"w":"discriminative.","start":166.083},{"w":"It","start":167.083},{"w":"learns","start":167.323},{"w":"the","start":167.723},{"w":"boundary","start":167.883},{"w":"between","start":168.403},{"w":"the","start":168.683},{"w":"classes,","start":168.803},{"w":"not","start":169.403},{"w":"how","start":169.683},{"w":"the","start":169.883},{"w":"data","start":170.083},{"w":"is","start":170.483},{"w":"made.","start":170.723}]}],"title":"Quick quiz","shortTitle":"Quick quiz: artist or judge?","props":{"kicker":"Pause and predict","question":"A model learns only the line that separates spam from normal mail. What kind is it?","options":["Generative","Discriminative"],"answer":1,"explain":"It learns the boundary, not how emails are written."},"start":152.067,"end":171.011,"startFrame":4562,"durationInFrames":596},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","component":"Recap","short":false,"cues":[{"id":"c24","start":171.933,"end":176.108,"marks":{},"text":"Let's recap. Generative models learn how each class's data is made.","words":[{"w":"Let's","start":171.933},{"w":"recap.","start":172.293},{"w":"Generative","start":172.973},{"w":"models","start":173.573},{"w":"learn","start":174.013},{"w":"how","start":174.253},{"w":"each","start":174.453},{"w":"class's","start":174.733},{"w":"data","start":175.213},{"w":"is","start":175.613},{"w":"made.","start":175.813}]},{"id":"c25","start":176.508,"end":179.919,"marks":{},"text":"Discriminative models learn the boundary between classes.","words":[{"w":"Discriminative","start":176.508},{"w":"models","start":177.348},{"w":"learn","start":177.948},{"w":"the","start":178.268},{"w":"boundary","start":178.388},{"w":"between","start":178.948},{"w":"classes.","start":179.268}]},{"id":"c26","start":180.319,"end":188.304,"marks":{},"text":"Naive Bayes and hidden Markov models are generative. Logistic regression and conditional random fields are discriminative.","words":[{"w":"Naive","start":180.319},{"w":"Bayes","start":180.819},{"w":"and","start":181.319},{"w":"hidden","start":181.519},{"w":"Markov","start":181.879},{"w":"models","start":182.319},{"w":"are","start":182.839},{"w":"generative.","start":183.039},{"w":"Logistic","start":184.079},{"w":"regression","start":184.719},{"w":"and","start":185.439},{"w":"conditional","start":185.679},{"w":"random","start":186.279},{"w":"fields","start":186.719},{"w":"are","start":187.199},{"w":"discriminative.","start":187.399}]},{"id":"c27","start":188.704,"end":193.22,"marks":{},"text":"And with plenty of labelled data, discriminative models usually come out ahead.","words":[{"w":"And","start":188.704},{"w":"with","start":188.824},{"w":"plenty","start":188.984},{"w":"of","start":189.384},{"w":"labelled","start":189.544},{"w":"data,","start":190.024},{"w":"discriminative","start":190.584},{"w":"models","start":191.424},{"w":"usually","start":191.984},{"w":"come","start":192.424},{"w":"out","start":192.664},{"w":"ahead.","start":192.824}]},{"id":"c28","start":193.62,"end":199.092,"marks":{},"text":"Next lesson: Hidden Markov Models. How can we guess hidden tags from the words we can see?","words":[{"w":"Next","start":193.94},{"w":"lesson:","start":194.26},{"w":"Hidden","start":194.58},{"w":"Markov","start":195.02},{"w":"Models.","start":195.62},{"w":"How","start":196.26},{"w":"can","start":196.5},{"w":"we","start":196.66},{"w":"guess","start":196.78},{"w":"hidden","start":197.06},{"w":"tags","start":197.38},{"w":"from","start":197.82},{"w":"the","start":197.98},{"w":"words","start":198.1},{"w":"we","start":198.42},{"w":"can","start":198.58},{"w":"see?","start":198.78}]}],"title":"Recap and next lesson","shortTitle":"Recap and next lesson","props":{"title":"Recap","points":["Generative: learn how each class's data is made","Discriminative: learn the boundary between classes","Naive Bayes and HMMs are generative; logistic regression and CRFs are discriminative","Lots of labelled data usually favours discriminative"],"next":{"title":"Hidden Markov Models","question":"How can we guess hidden tags from the words we can see?"}},"start":171.933,"end":199.092,"startFrame":5158,"durationInFrames":860}],"refs":{"c01":0.6,"c02":8.67,"c03":18.433,"c04":27.733,"c05":36.757,"c06":45.658,"c07":54.367,"c08":61.944,"c09":69.079,"c10":75.533,"c11":77.595,"c12":87.946,"c13":100.067,"c14":103.685,"c15":108.044,"c16":114.582,"c17":119.683,"c18":126.833,"c19":129.686,"c20":140.607,"c21":152.067,"c22":160.883,"c23":165.803,"c24":171.933,"c25":176.508,"c26":180.319,"c27":188.704,"c28":193.62},"audio":[{"file":".cache/proc/0031a5dd05faf316.wav","start":0.6},{"file":".cache/proc/b1b6e1a8fa198fc8.wav","start":8.67},{"file":".cache/proc/03af32092fed6001.wav","start